# DTF tier manifest: pypi-metadata-gate-index-fetch
#   (artifact-keeper#6439, artifact-keeper#5983, artifact-keeper#6444, 1.13.0)
# Profile-set: filesystem + upstreams.mock-files (a stdlib-python upstream at
# 172.16.<slot>.76 whose files the oracle sets over its control API on the
# slot's TRIVY_PORT; it serves a file gzip-coded to a request that asks for
# gzip when stored with gzip=1, a PEP 691 JSON variant to a JSON Accept, and
# counts each path's GETs, body bytes and Accept-Encoding).
#
# THE BUGS
# #6439  PyPI PEP 658 `.metadata` skipped the download gate its distribution
#        passes. A hosted row that was quarantined or rejected answered 409 /
#        403 for the wheel and 200 for its METADATA, directly and through a
#        virtual. In a virtual whose combined scan policy scans over a Remote
#        member that does not (#3023), the wheel answered 403 on its stored
#        vulnerable verdict while `.metadata` answered 200: from the member's
#        proxy-cache row when it had one, from the member's upstream sidecar
#        when it did not.
# #5983  PyPI metadata resolution: the client-facing simple index was fetched
#        from the upstream as identity (about 5x the bytes of gzip for a large
#        index), and a cold `.whl.metadata` read the whole upstream index again
#        to find the sidecar's URL even though the client had just been served
#        that index from the proxy cache.
# #6444  Under fail_closed, the `.metadata` of a cached wheel with no verdict
#        whose cached copy was stale went through the wheel's own download
#        path: it re-read the index and downloaded the whole wheel to answer
#        a request for a few KB of METADATA.
#
# THE GATES
#   (CONTROL) both images: an open hosted wheel and its metadata answer 200;
#     a held / rejected hosted wheel answers 409 / 403; the scanning virtual
#     refuses the member's wheel (403, or 423 if it re-grades); the member
#     Remote's own route (it does not scan) serves the metadata; four
#     concurrent cold sidecars of a project whose index was never fetched
#     share one upstream index read (#6047); a release published upstream
#     after the index was cached resolves, with the new sidecar's bytes.
#   (FIX) #6439: the held / rejected hosted wheel's metadata answers 409 / 403
#     directly and through a virtual; through the scanning virtual the
#     member's wheel metadata answers 403, with and without a row (GET and
#     HEAD); a cached member wheel with no verdict while the CVE engine is
#     down (grype stubbed) gets the wheel's own answer (423) for its
#     metadata; a version the download age gate withholds answers 451 for
#     its metadata (GET and HEAD). RED on main: 200.
#   (FIX) #5983: the client-facing index (JSON, as uv asks, and HTML, as pip
#     asks) is asked for gzip; after it, four concurrent cold sidecars of the
#     project read no upstream index (1 index read in all, 2 on main). The
#     oracle prints the index bytes on the wire and the sidecars' wall time
#     for the before/after numbers.
#   (FIX) #6444: a Remote caches wheels and their indexes while it does
#     not scan (real upstreams: the sidecar is the wheel's METADATA and the
#     index advertises its hash); the wheels' sidecars are aged on disk and
#     the Remote switched to fail_closed. Their `.metadata` fetches the wheel
#     from the upstream 0 times (1 on main) and answers what the wheel
#     answers, GET and HEAD: a clean wheel (its sha256 is the index's
#     core-metadata), jinja2 2.10 whose cached bytes grade vulnerable (403),
#     a clean wheel through a virtual. pip downloads a stale wheel through
#     its metadata. When the upstream reuses a cached file name for other
#     bytes, the metadata is 503 with Retry-After until the wheel's own
#     request has gated the new bytes, then the new METADATA. MEASURE lines
#     print the counts.
#
# Red: main before the fix. Green: an image carrying artifact-keeper#6439,
# artifact-keeper#5983 and artifact-keeper#6444.
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: requests back to back.
# UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS=true: the mock upstream is on a private subnet.
COVERS="6439 5983 6444"
PROFILES="storage.filesystem upstreams.mock-files"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
UPSTREAM_ALLOW_PRIVATE_IPS="true"
